            "duration": "15 mins",
            "type": "text",
            "content": "### Introduction to Fractions & Percentages\n\n- Fractions represent parts of a whole.\n- Percentages represent values out of 100.\n- Both are used in daily life calculations.\n- Important in AI data representation.\n- Help in comparisons.\n- Widely used in statistics.\n\n> **Example:** Half pizza = 1/2, 50% = half.\n\n---\n\n### Understanding Fractions\n\n- Fraction has numerator and denominator.\n- Numerator = top number.\n- Denominator = bottom number.\n- Represents division of parts.\n- Can be simplified.\n- Used in measurements.\n\n> **Example:** 3/4 means 3 parts out of 4.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Fractions\n\n- Proper fractions (numerator < denominator).\n- Improper fractions (numerator > denominator).\n- Mixed fractions (whole + fraction).\n- Each type has different use.\n- Can be converted.\n- Used in math problems.\n\n> **Example:** 1 1/2 is a mixed fraction.\n\n---\n\n### Understanding Percentages\n\n- Percentage means “out of 100”.\n- Used to compare values.\n- Easy way to express proportions.\n- Common in exams and finance.\n- Important in AI data analysis.\n- Simple conversion from fractions.\n\n> **Example:** 75% means 75 out of 100.\n\n---\n\n### Fractions and Percent Conversion\n\n- Fraction to percentage: multiply by 100.\n- Percentage to fraction: divide by 100.\n- Helps in calculations.\n- Used in data analysis.\n- Easy conversions.\n- Important in programming.\n\n> **Example:** 1/2 = 50%\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- Exam marks calculation.\n- Discounts in shopping.\n- Bank interest rates.\n- AI data representation.\n- Statistics and reports.\n- Performance analysis.\n\n> **Example:** 20% discount on a product.",
