            "title": "Vectors",
            "duration": "15 mins",
            "type": "text",
            "content": "### Introduction to Vectors\n\n- A vector is a quantity that has both magnitude and direction.\n- Used to represent data in AI and mathematics.\n- Can be 2D, 3D, or higher dimensions.\n- Important in machine learning and graphics.\n- Helps represent real-world data.\n- Foundation of linear algebra.\n\n> **Example:** Direction of a moving car (speed + direction).\n\n---\n\n### Representation of Vectors\n\n- Written in column or row form.\n- \n\n> **Example:** (x, y) or (x, y, z).\n- Each value is called a component.\n- Represents position or direction.\n- Used in calculations.\n- Easy to visualize in geometry.\n\n> **Example:** (3, 4) represents movement on a plane.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Vectors\n\n- Zero vector (no magnitude).\n- Unit vector (magnitude = 1).\n- Position vector (location in space).\n- Row vector and column vector.\n- Each type has a different use.\n- Used in AI models.\n\n> **Example:** Unit vector used in direction modeling.\n\n---\n\n### Vectors in AI\n\n- Represent data points in datasets.\n- Used in image and text processing.\n- Help in machine learning models.\n- Store features of data.\n- Enable similarity calculations.\n- Core concept in AI.\n\n> **Example:** Each user in Netflix is represented as a vector.\n\n---\n\n### Operations on Vectors\n\n- Addition and subtraction.\n- Scalar multiplication.\n- Dot product.\n- Used to combine or compare data.\n- Important in calculations.\n- Used in AI algorithms.\n\n> **Example:** Combining two feature sets in AI.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications of Vectors\n\n- GPS navigation systems.\n- Computer graphics and games.\n- AI recommendation systems.\n- Robotics movement.\n- Physics simulations.\n- Data science models.\n\n> **Example:** Google Maps showing direction and distance.",
