            "duration": "15 mins",
            "type": "text",
            "content": "### Introduction to Linear Regression\n\n- Linear Regression is a supervised learning algorithm.\n- It predicts continuous numerical values.\n- Finds relationship between variables.\n- Based on straight-line equation.\n- Simple and widely used algorithm.\n- Core concept in ML.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting house price based on size.\n\n---\n\n### How Linear Regression Works\n\n- Finds best-fit line through data.\n- Uses equation: Y = mX + c.\n- Minimizes prediction error.\n- Learns from training data.\n- Used for forecasting.\n- Improves with more data.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting salary from experience years.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Linear Regression\n\n- Simple linear regression (one variable).\n- Multiple linear regression (multiple variables).\n- Each used for different problems.\n- Helps model relationships.\n- Common in AI systems.\n- Easy to implement.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting marks using study hours + attendance.\n\n---\n\n### Advantages of Linear Regression\n\n- Simple and easy to understand.\n- Fast to train.\n- Works well for linear data.\n- Interpretable results.\n- Widely used in AI.\n- Good for predictions.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting sales based on advertising budget.\n\n---\n\n### Limitations\n\n- Works only with linear data.\n- Sensitive to outliers.\n- Not good for complex patterns.\n- Limited accuracy in some cases.\n- Needs clean data.\n- Simple model only.\n\n> **Example:** Fails in nonlinear stock market prediction.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- House price prediction.\n- Salary forecasting.\n- Sales prediction.\n- Weather trends.\n- Economic analysis.\n- AI forecasting systems.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting electricity usage over time.",
