            "content": "### Introduction to KNN\n\n- KNN is a supervised learning algorithm.\n- Used for classification and regression.\n- Based on similarity between data points.\n- “K” means number of neighbors.\n- Simple and intuitive algorithm.\n- Used in pattern recognition.\n\n> **Example:** Classifying a fruit based on similar fruits.\n\n---\n\n### How KNN Works\n\n- Finds nearest data points.\n- Checks majority class among neighbors.\n- Assigns most common class.\n- Distance-based algorithm.\n- No training phase needed.\n- Works on similarity.\n\n> **Example:** Grouping similar students based on marks.\n\n---\n\n### Choosing K Value\n\n- K = number of neighbors.\n- Small K = sensitive to noise.\n- Large K = smoother results.\n- Important for accuracy.\n- Must be carefully selected.\n- Affects model performance.\n\n> **Example:** K = 3 or K = 5 commonly used.\n\n---\n\n### Advantages of KNN\n\n- Simple and easy to implement.\n- No training phase required.\n- Works well with small datasets.\n- Flexible algorithm.\n- Good for classification.\n- Intuitive approach.\n\n> **Example:** Face recognition based on similarity.\n\n---\n\n### Limitations of KNN\n\n- Slow for large datasets.\n- Needs high memory.\n- Sensitive to noise.\n- Not good for high dimensions.\n- Requires distance calculation.\n- Less efficient for big data.\n\n> **Example:** Slow performance with millions of records.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- Recommendation systems.\n- Image recognition.\n- Pattern recognition.\n- Medical diagnosis.\n- Fraud detection.\n- Text classification.\n\n> **Example:** Suggesting similar movies on Netflix.",
