            "content": "### Introduction to Simple Calculator AI\n\n- A Simple Calculator AI performs basic math operations.\n- It uses rules or logic-based programming.\n- Can handle addition, subtraction, multiplication, division.\n- First step in learning AI logic building.\n- Does not require complex machine learning.\n- Very beginner-friendly project.\n\n> **Example:** AI calculating 10 + 5 = 15 automatically.\n\n---\n\n### How It Works\n\n- User inputs a mathematical expression.\n- System identifies operation type.\n- Applies predefined rules.\n- Performs calculation step-by-step.\n- Returns final result.\n- Uses simple logic structures.\n\n> **Example:** Input: 8 × 2 → Output: 16\n\n---\n\n### Key Features\n\n- Supports basic arithmetic operations.\n- Fast and accurate calculations.\n- Rule-based decision system.\n- Easy to implement in Python or Java.\n- No training data required.\n- Beginner AI project.\n\n> **Example:** A mobile calculator app with smart input detection.\n\n---\n\n### Why It Is Useful\n\n- Helps understand AI logic flow.\n- Builds programming confidence.\n- Foundation for advanced AI systems.\n- Teaches decision-making logic.\n- Improves coding skills.\n- Easy practice project.\n\n> **Example:** Students learning AI logic using calculator app.\n\n---\n\n### Limitations\n\n- Cannot solve complex equations.\n- No learning capability.\n- Limited to predefined rules.\n- Not adaptive like ML models.\n- Basic functionality only.\n- No intelligence improvement.\n\n> **Example:** Cannot solve algebraic expressions automatically.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- Mobile calculator apps.\n- Embedded systems calculators.\n- Educational tools.\n- Basic AI logic systems.\n- Smart assistants (basic math).\n- Web-based calculators.\n\n> **Example:** Google calculator solving quick math.",
