            "duration": "15 mins",
            "type": "text",
            "content": "### Introduction to Rule-based Chatbot\n\n- A rule-based chatbot follows predefined rules.\n- It responds based on keywords or patterns.\n- Does not learn from data.\n- Simple form of AI system.\n- Used in beginner AI projects.\n- Easy to design and implement.\n\n> **Example:** Chatbot replying “Hello” when user says “Hi”.\n\n---\n\n### How It Works\n\n- User sends a message.\n- System checks keywords.\n- Matches rule from database.\n- Generates predefined response.\n- Sends reply to user.\n- Works like decision tree.\n\n> **Example:** “What is your name?” → “I am AI bot.”\n\n---\n\n### Key Features\n\n- Uses if-else logic rules.\n- No machine learning required.\n- Fast response system.\n- Easy to build.\n- Works with fixed responses.\n- Beginner-friendly AI project.\n\n> **Example:** FAQ chatbot for school website.\n\n---\n\n### Advantages\n\n- Simple and easy to develop.\n- No training data needed.\n- Fast response time.\n- Low computational cost.\n- Useful for basic automation.\n- Reliable for fixed queries.\n\n> **Example:** Customer support FAQ bot.\n\n---\n\n### Limitations\n\n- Cannot understand complex language.\n- No learning ability.\n- Limited responses.\n- Cannot handle unknown questions.\n- Poor flexibility.\n- Requires manual updates.\n\n> **Example:** Fails if user asks unexpected question.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- Customer service bots.\n- Educational FAQ systems.\n- Website assistants.\n- Banking support chatbots.\n- E-commerce help systems.\n- Simple AI assistants.\n\n> **Example:** Website chatbot answering delivery questions.",
