    "id": "top-ai-2-2-2",
    "title": "Variables and Data Types",
    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Variables\n\n- Variables store data values.\n- Used to hold information in programs.\n- Can change during execution.\n- No need to declare type in Python.\n- Easy to use.\n- Important for programming.\n\n> **Example:** x = 10 stores value 10.\n\n---\n\n### Rules for Naming Variables\n\n- Must start with a letter or underscore.\n- Cannot start with a number.\n- Cannot use keywords.\n- Case-sensitive.\n- Use meaningful names.\n- Avoid spaces.\n\n> **Example:** age = 20 is valid, 2age is not.\n\n---\n\n### Data Types in Python\n\n- Integer (int): whole numbers.\n- Float: decimal numbers.\n- String: text data.\n- Boolean: True or False.\n- Each type has a purpose.\n- Used to represent different data.\n\n> **Example:** name = \"Ali\" (string).\n\n---\n\n### Type Checking and Conversion\n\n- Use type() to check type.\n- Convert using int(), float(), str().\n- Helps in data handling.\n- Prevents errors.\n- Useful in user input.\n- Makes programs flexible.\n\n> **Example:** int(\"5\") converts string to integer.\n\n---\n\n### Using Variables in Programs\n\n- Variables store user input.\n- Used in calculations.\n- Can be updated anytime.\n- Helps manage data easily.\n- Improves code readability.\n- Essential for logic building.\n\n> **Example:** total = price + tax\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Use of Data Types\n\n- Integer for counting items.\n- Float for measurements.\n- String for names.\n- Boolean for decisions.\n- Used in apps and systems.\n- Important in AI data processing.\n\n> **Example:** is_passed = True",
