    "id": "top-ai-2-3-2",
    "title": "Loops (for, while)",
    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Loops\n\n- Loops are used to repeat tasks automatically.\n- Save time and reduce code repetition.\n- Useful for repetitive operations.\n- Two main types: for and while.\n- Improve efficiency.\n- Common in all programs.\n\n> **Example:** Printing numbers from 1 to 10.\n\n---\n\n### for Loop\n\n- Used when number of iterations is known.\n- Iterates over a sequence.\n- Simple and easy to use.\n- Common in Python.\n- Works with lists and ranges.\n- Improves readability.\n\n> **Example:**\n- for i in range(1,6):\n- print(i)\n\n---\n\n### while Loop\n\n- Runs as long as condition is True.\n- Used when iterations are unknown.\n- Depends on condition.\n- Can run indefinitely if not controlled.\n- Requires careful handling.\n- Useful in many cases.\n\n> **Example:**\n- i = 1\n- while i <= 5:\n- print(i)\n- i += 1\n\n---\n\n### Loop Control (break, continue)\n\n- break stops loop completely.\n- continue skips current iteration.\n- Helps control loop behavior.\n- Improves efficiency.\n- Useful in conditions.\n- Makes loops flexible.\n\n> **Example:**\n- for i in range(5):\n- if i == 3:\n- break\n\n---\n\n### Nested Loops\n\n- Loop inside another loop.\n- Used for complex patterns.\n- Increases power of loops.\n- Can be time-consuming.\n- Useful for grids and tables.\n- Requires careful design.\n\n> **Example:** Printing multiplication table.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Use of Loops\n\n- Processing large data sets.\n- Repeating tasks in apps.\n- Game development loops.\n- AI training iterations.\n- Automation scripts.\n- Data analysis tasks.\n\n> **Example:** Checking each item in a shopping list.",
