    "id": "top-ai-2-3-3",
    "title": "Functions",
    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Functions\n\n- Functions are reusable blocks of code.\n- Help organize programs.\n- Avoid code repetition.\n- Make programs modular.\n- Improve readability.\n- Essential in large programs.\n\n> **Example:** Function to calculate sum of two numbers.\n\n---\n\n### Defining a Function\n\n- Use def keyword.\n- Give function a name.\n- Write code inside function.\n- Indentation is important.\n- Can be called anytime.\n- Simplifies code.\n\n> **Example:**\n- def greet():\n- print(\"Hello\")\n\n---\n\n### Function with Parameters\n\n- Accepts input values.\n- Makes function flexible.\n- Can pass different data.\n- Improves usability.\n- Used in most programs.\n- Easy to understand.\n\n> **Example:**\n- def greet(name):\n- print(\"Hello\", name)\n\n---\n\n### Return Statement\n\n- Sends value back from function.\n- Used for calculations.\n- Makes functions useful.\n- Can return multiple values.\n- Important in programming.\n- Helps reuse results.\n\n> **Example:**\n- def add(a,b):\n- return a+b\n\n---\n\n### Types of Functions\n\n- Built-in functions (print, len).\n- User-defined functions.\n- Functions with/without parameters.\n- Functions with/without return.\n- Each type has purpose.\n- Used in different cases.\n\n> **Example:** len(\"AI\") returns length.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Use of Functions\n\n- Used in all software systems.\n- Break complex tasks into parts.\n- Improve code management.\n- Reuse logic multiple times.\n- Essential in AI programs.\n- Used in automation.\n\n> **Example:** Login function used in multiple apps.",
