    "content": "### Introduction to Algebra\n\n- Algebra uses letters to represent numbers.\n- Letters are called variables.\n- Helps solve unknown values.\n- Important in mathematics and AI.\n- Used in formulas.\n- Builds logical thinking.\n\n> **Example:** x + 5 = 10\n\n---\n\n### Variables in Algebra\n\n- Variables represent unknown values.\n- Common symbols: x, y, z.\n- Can change values.\n- Used in equations.\n- Important in programming.\n- Helps solve problems.\n\n> **Example:** x = 3 in equation x + 2 = 5\n\n---\n\n### Simple Algebraic Expressions\n\n- Combination of numbers and variables.\n- Includes operations like +, -, ×.\n- No equality sign in expressions.\n- Used in formulas.\n- Easy to evaluate.\n- Foundation of algebra.\n\n> **Example:** 2x + 3\n\n---\n\n### Solving Simple Equations\n\n- Find value of unknown variable.\n- Use inverse operations.\n- Keep equation balanced.\n- Step-by-step solving.\n- Important in logic building.\n- Used in AI algorithms.\n\n> **Example:** x + 2 = 5 → x = 3\n\n---\n\n### Algebra in Programming\n\n- Used in formulas and logic.\n- Helps in calculations.\n- Used in AI models.\n- Represents real-world problems.\n- Easy to implement in code.\n- Supports decision-making.\n\n> **Example:** x = a + b in Python\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- Engineering calculations.\n- AI model training.\n- Financial analysis.\n- Data prediction systems.\n- Game development.\n- Problem-solving tasks.\n\n> **Example:** Calculating profit using formula.",
