    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Matrices\n\n- A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of numbers.\n- Organized in rows and columns.\n- Used to represent data in structured form.\n- Important in AI and mathematics.\n- Helps in solving complex problems.\n- Foundation of linear algebra.\n\n> **Example:** Table of student marks in rows and columns.\n\n---\n\n### Structure of a Matrix\n\n- Rows = horizontal lines.\n- Columns = vertical lines.\n- Each element has a position (i, j).\n- Can be square or rectangular.\n- Size defined as rows × columns.\n- Easy to organize data.\n\n> **Example:** 2×3 matrix has 2 rows and 3 columns.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Matrices\n\n- Row matrix (1 row).\n- Column matrix (1 column).\n- Square matrix (equal rows and columns).\n- Zero matrix (all elements zero).\n- Identity matrix (diagonal = 1).\n- Used in different applications.\n\n> **Example:** Identity matrix used in transformations.\n\n---\n\n### Matrices in AI\n\n- Store large datasets.\n- Represent images (pixels as matrix).\n- Used in neural networks.\n- Help in computations.\n- Enable pattern recognition.\n- Core part of machine learning.\n\n> **Example:** Image stored as pixel matrix in AI systems.\n\n---\n\n### Advantages of Matrices\n\n- Organize complex data.\n- Simplify calculations.\n- Used in multiple dimensions.\n- Improve computational efficiency.\n- Essential in AI models.\n- Support mathematical operations.\n\n> **Example:** Face recognition using matrix data.\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications of Matrices\n\n- Computer graphics.\n- Image processing.\n- Robotics systems.\n- AI and machine learning.\n- Engineering simulations.\n- Data analysis systems.\n\n> **Example:** Photoshop using matrix operations for image editing.",
