    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Data Distribution\n\n- Data distribution shows how data is spread.\n- Helps understand dataset structure.\n- Important in statistics and AI.\n- Used in data visualization.\n- Helps identify patterns.\n- Essential for machine learning.\n\n> **Example:** Exam scores distribution in class\n\n---\n\n### Types of Data Distribution\n\n- Normal distribution (bell curve).\n- Skewed distribution (left/right bias).\n- Uniform distribution (equal spread).\n- Each type shows different pattern.\n- Used in data analysis.\n- Important in AI modeling.\n\n> **Example:** Heights of students (normal distribution)\n\n---\n\n### Normal Distribution\n\n- Symmetrical bell-shaped curve.\n- Mean = Median = Mode.\n- Most data near center.\n- Common in natural data.\n- Used in statistics and AI.\n- Very important concept.\n\n> **Example:** Human height distribution\n\n---\n\n### Skewed Distribution\n\n- Data is not symmetrical.\n- Can be left or right skewed.\n- Mean, median, mode differ.\n- Common in real-world data.\n- Used in financial analysis.\n- Important for AI correction.\n\n> **Example:** Income distribution in a country\n\n---\n\n### Importance in AI\n\n- Helps understand data patterns.\n- Improves model accuracy.\n- Used in preprocessing data.\n- Detects anomalies.\n- Supports decision-making.\n- Essential for ML training.\n\n> **Example:** Detecting unusual transactions in banking data\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Applications\n\n- Stock market analysis.\n- Weather pattern prediction.\n- Customer behavior analysis.\n- AI model training datasets.\n- Medical data interpretation.\n- Business forecasting.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting sales trends using past data",
