    "title": "Matplotlib Basics",
    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Matplotlib\n\n- Matplotlib is a Python library for data visualization.\n- Used to create graphs and charts.\n- Very popular in data science.\n- Helps visualize complex data.\n- Works with arrays and datasets.\n- Core tool for AI analysis.\n\n> **Example:** Plotting a simple line graph in Python.\n\n---\n\n### Why Matplotlib is Used\n\n- Easy to create graphs.\n- Highly customizable.\n- Works with NumPy and Pandas.\n- Supports many chart types.\n- Widely used in AI projects.\n- Beginner-friendly.\n\n> **Example:** Visualizing student marks in a graph.\n\n---\n\n### Basic Plot Example\n\n- Use plt.plot() to draw graphs.\n- Define X and Y values.\n- Add labels and titles.\n- Show graph using plt.show().\n- Simple syntax.\n- Powerful output.\n\n> **Example:** Plotting numbers 1–5 against squares.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Plots in Matplotlib\n\n- Line plot.\n- Bar plot.\n- Scatter plot.\n- Histogram.\n- Pie chart.\n- Each used for different data types.\n\n> **Example:** Bar plot for exam results.\n\n---\n\n### Customizing Graphs\n\n- Add titles and labels.\n- Change colors and styles.\n- Add legends.\n- Improve readability.\n- Make professional charts.\n- Used in reports.\n\n> **Example:** Adding title “Student Performance Graph”.\n\n---\n\n### Matplotlib in AI\n\n- Used for data analysis.\n- Helps visualize model results.\n- Important in debugging AI models.\n- Used in research.\n- Supports data understanding.\n- Essential for ML workflows.\n\n> **Example:** Visualizing training accuracy over time.",
