    "content": "### Introduction to Seaborn\n\n- Seaborn is a Python data visualization library.\n- Built on top of Matplotlib.\n- Provides attractive statistical graphics.\n- Easier to use than Matplotlib.\n- Used in data analysis.\n- Common in AI projects.\n\n> **Example:** Creating a colorful bar chart easily.\n\n---\n\n### Why Seaborn is Used\n\n- Beautiful default styles.\n- Simple syntax.\n- Works with Pandas dataframes.\n- Better for statistical plots.\n- Saves coding time.\n- Widely used in data science.\n\n> **Example:** Heatmap for correlation analysis.\n\n---\n\n### Common Seaborn Plots\n\n- Bar plot.\n- Line plot.\n- Heatmap.\n- Pair plot.\n- Box plot.\n- Violin plot.\n\n> **Example:** Heatmap showing student score correlations.\n\n---\n\n### Heatmaps in Seaborn\n\n- Displays data in color matrix.\n- Shows relationships clearly.\n- Used in correlation analysis.\n- Easy to interpret.\n- Popular in AI.\n- Very powerful visualization.\n\n> **Example:** Correlation between subjects marks.\n\n---\n\n### Seaborn vs Matplotlib\n\n- Seaborn = easier and prettier.\n- Matplotlib = more control.\n- Seaborn built on Matplotlib.\n- Both used together.\n- Seaborn for statistics.\n- Matplotlib for customization.\n\n> **Example:** Seaborn heatmap vs Matplotlib line graph.\n\n---\n\n### Seaborn in AI\n\n- Used in data exploration.\n- Helps understand datasets.\n- Used in preprocessing stage.\n- Improves data insights.\n- Common in ML workflows.\n- Supports decision-making.\n\n> **Example:** Visualizing customer behavior patterns.",
