    "duration": "15 mins",
    "type": "text",
    "content": "### Introduction to Problem Definition\n\n- Problem definition is the first step in ML workflow.\n- It identifies what we want to solve using AI.\n- Clearly defines input and output.\n- Helps set goals for the model.\n- Important for project success.\n- Avoids confusion in development.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting whether a student will pass or fail.\n\n---\n\n### Why Problem Definition is Important\n\n- Gives direction to ML project.\n- Helps choose correct algorithm.\n- Defines success criteria.\n- Saves time and effort.\n- Improves model accuracy.\n- Guides data collection.\n\n> **Example:** Defining “spam email detection” problem clearly.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Problems in ML\n\n- Classification problems.\n- Regression problems.\n- Clustering problems.\n- Each requires different approach.\n- Depends on data type.\n- Used in different AI systems.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting house price vs spam detection.\n\n---\n\n### Understanding Inputs and Outputs\n\n- Input = data given to model.\n- Output = result predicted by model.\n- Must be clearly defined.\n- Helps structure ML system.\n- Essential for training.\n- Used in all AI models.\n\n> **Example:** Input: student marks → Output: pass/fail\n\n---\n\n### Real-Life Problem Definition\n\n- Business problem analysis.\n- Healthcare diagnosis systems.\n- Fraud detection systems.\n- Recommendation engines.\n- Weather forecasting.\n- AI chatbot design.\n\n> **Example:** Predicting customer churn in a company.\n\n---\n\n### Impact in AI Projects\n\n- Strong problem definition improves accuracy.\n- Weak definition leads to failure.\n- Guides entire ML pipeline.\n- Essential for success.\n- Used in real AI systems.\n- First step in every project.\n\n> **Example:** Clearly defining self-driving car detection task.",
